surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Rost</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Baldi</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Improving the Prediction of Protein Secondary Structure in Three and Eight Classes Using Neural Networks and Profiles</article-title><source>Proteins</source><year>2002</year><volume>47</volume><fpage>228</fpage><lpage>235</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11933069</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/prot.10082</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rost</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Review: Protein Secondary Structure Prediction Continues to Rise</article-title><source>J Struct Biol</source><year>2001</year><volume>134</volume><fpage>204</fpage><lpage>218</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11551180</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1006/jsbi.2001.4336</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Armano</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Mancosu</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Orro</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A MultiAgent System for Protein Secondary Structure Prediction</article-title><source>NETTAB 2004: Models and Metaphors from Biology to Bioinformatics Tools</source><year>2004</year></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kawashima</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Ogata</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Kanehisa</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>AAindex: Amino Acid Index Database</article-title><source>Nucleic Acids Research</source><year>1999</year><volume>27</volume><fpage>368</fpage><lpage>369</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9847231</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/nar/27.1.368</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wilson</surname><given-names>SW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Classifier Fitness Based on Accuracy</article-title><source>Evolutionary Computation</source><year>1995</year><volume>3</volume><fpage>149</fpage><lpage>175</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Berman</surname><given-names>HM</given-names></name><name><surname>Westbrook</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Feng</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Gilliland</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Bhat</surname><given-names>TN</given-names></name><name><surname>Weissig</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Shindyalov</surname><given-names>IN</given-names></name><name><surname>Bourne</surname><given-names>PE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Protein Data Bank</article-title><source>Nucleic Acids Research</source><year>2000</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>235</fpage><lpage>242</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10592235</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/nar/28.1.235</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zemla</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Vencolvas</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Fidelis</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Rost</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A modified definition of SOV, a segment-based measure for protein secondary structure prediction assesment</article-title><source>Proteins</source><year>1999</year><volume>34</volume><fpage>220</fpage><lpage>223</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10022357</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/(SICI)1097-0134(19990201)34:2&#x0003c;220::AID-PROT7&#x0003e;3.0.CO;2-K</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>The micro-architecture of an expert.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-6-S4-S3-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>The architecture of the module that performs the sequence-to-structure processing.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-6-S4-S3-2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>The pseudo-code of the "soft-partitioning" algorithm (LS = learning set).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2105-6-S4-S3-3"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Features used for "soft" partitioning the input space (r denotes the length of the corresponding window)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="center"><bold>Feature</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Conjecture</bold></td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="center">1 Check whether hydrophobic aminoacids occur in the current window (r = 15) according to a clear periodicity (i.e., one every 3&#x02013;4 residues)</td><td align="center">Alpha helices may sometimes fulfill this pattern</td></tr><tr><td align="center">2 Check whether the current window (r = 13) contains numerous residues in {A,E,L,M} and few residues in {P,G,Y,S}</td><td align="center">Alpha helices are often evidenced by {A,E,L,M} residues, whereas {P,G,Y,S} residues account for their absence</td></tr><tr><td align="center">3 Check whether the left side of the current window (r = 13) is mostly hydrophobic and the right part is mostly hydrophilic (and viceversa)</td><td align="center">Transmembrane alpha helices may fulfil this feature</td></tr><tr><td align="center">4 Check whether, on the average, the current window (r = 11) is positively charged or not</td><td align="center">A positive charge might account for alpha helices or beta sheets</td></tr><tr><td align="center">5 Check whether, on the average, the current window (r = 11) is negatively charged or not</td><td align="center">A negative charge might account for alpha helices or beta sheets</td></tr><tr><td align="center">6 Check whether, on the average, the current window (r = 11) is neutral</td><td align="center">A neutral charge might account for coils</td></tr><tr><td align="center">7 Check whether the current window (r = 11) mostly contains \small" residues</td><td align="center">Small residues might account for alpha helices or beta sheets</td></tr><tr><td align="center">8 Check whether the current window (r = 11) mostly contains polar residues</td><td align="center">Polar residues might account for alpha helices or beta sheets</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Results obtained on the RS126 test set in accordance with the training strategies and encodingtechniques that have been experimented</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="center"><bold>Experiment</bold></td><td align="left"><bold>Accuracy</bold></td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="center">Random population</td><td align="left">69.1</td></tr><tr><td align="center">Genetically-selected population</td><td align="left">71.8</td></tr><tr><td align="center">Improved experts' specialization technique (global + local)</td><td align="left">73.2</td></tr><tr><td align="center">Using PSI-BLAST profiles</td><td align="left">74.7</td></tr><tr><td align="center">Using post-processing</td><td align="left">76.1</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T3"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Detailed results obtained on the RS126 test set using PSI-BLAST profiles and post-processing</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td></td><td align="left"><bold>&#x003b1;</bold></td><td align="left"><bold>&#x003b2;</bold></td><td align="left"><bold>c</bold></td><td align="left"><bold>overall</bold></td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left"><bold>Accuracy</bold></td><td align="left">78.2</td><td align="left">63.1</td><td align="left">81.3</td><td align="left">76.1</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><bold>SOV</bold></td><td align="left">77.8</td><td align="left">73.2</td><td align="left">70.3</td><td align="left">71.8</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec></back></article>